      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart New York.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Intramedullary tumors affect a small but significant portion of patients with spinal tumors. Ependymomas and astrocytomas are the most common entities. The diagnosis of a mature teratoma is extremely rare, although not in the presence of associated developmental abnormalities. The medullary conus is the most common location. Such a case with caudal exophytic growth is presented here and the literature extensively reviewed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "CASE PRESENTATION",
            "content": "Low back pain and muscle weakness led to diagnosis of a exophytic intramedullary lesion of the conus medullaris in a 52-year-old woman. After subtotal resection the symptomatology partially improved. Histopathological examination revealed mature teratoma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Literature review identified 68 cases of intramedullary teratomas. These should be considered in the differential diagnosis when an associated developmental abnormality is present. Subtotal resection is a valid alternative to radical tumor removal when neurological function is at risk. The prognosis of adult patients with intramedullary mature teratoma is excellent."
          }
        ]
      },
